### D-93 · Faster sign-in offer: the phone can't do it at Turn on — 2026-11-09 (placement revised the same day)
- **Decision (Apurva):** if the phone answers "unavailable" when the doctor taps **Turn on** (e.g. fingerprints removed after the offer appeared), the offer screen **stays as it was**: title, subtitle, tile and the three points. The bar shows an **amber warning notice** ("Faster sign-in isn't available on this phone right now.", `unlock.unavailable`, `alert-triangle`) above a single primary **Continue** → Home. Not now is hidden. Faster sign-in is not enabled, and the offer is recorded as shown. New state `offer_unavailable` (390 / 360), plus one new label "Continue" (`offer.continue`). Approved concept: `docs/renders/2026-11-09-step1-recovery/session-5/d93/bar-proposal/`. Closes OQ-036.
- **Why:** it is the result of the Turn on tap, so under RULES §9.5 (A-19) the notice goes in the bar above the button, like the approved "Couldn't turn on" and Offline states. Warning, not error, because the phone is blocking the action rather than an action having failed. Keeping the screen unchanged shows what the doctor tried to turn on, so no content needs hiding. Neutral wording, because the phone gives no reason.
- **Rejected:** notice at the top of the panel (first D-93 version, rendered in session 5: that placement is for the screen's general situation, not the result of a tap); hiding the three points; a bottom sheet (sheets are for choices and confirmations, RULES §9.4, and a one-button sheet is just an alert); Try again (can't succeed); going to Home silently; "Biometrics is not enabled" (wrong in two of the three causes, and jargon); opening phone settings now (iOS can't open the Face ID / Touch ID page, and the web prototype can't open settings at all). Android "Set up on this phone" is logged in backlog §4b.

### D-94 · Approved pictures re-rendered on the new Mac (same designs) — 2026-11-09
- **Decision (Apurva):** after step 1, all 148 approved pictures are re-rendered from their approved sources on the new Mac, and screen captures are made repeatable. Each picture is checked to differ from the old one only by text-edge smoothing: no moved boxes, shifted rows or colour changes. The main thread reviews that evidence before the pictures replace the approved set. Then the anti-aliasing allow-list is removed, so app-vs-picture compares must match exactly, apart from listed, reasoned regions.
- **Why:** 128 pictures came from the old Mac and 20 (D-92) from the new one. The mix forced an anti-aliasing allow-list and capture retries, and that test debt would triple in step 2. No design changes.
- **Rejected:** keeping the allow-list (weaker test, and it grows with every step); refreshing during step 2 (mixes two kinds of change).

### D-96 · Native hand-off kit for the React Native team, piloted on Sign-in — 2026-11-09
- **Decision (Apurva):** the React Native team gets a **hand-off kit** in the repo (`handoff/`), not a token list or hand-written RN code. For people: foundation files (tokens, icons, messages, native fonts), a spec for each building block (parts × states → native token), a screen viewer (tap an element → block, tokens, spacing; "tokens used" table; raw values flagged red), behaviour links and a "what changed" list. For their AI: `AGENTS.md` + `CLAUDE.md`, a skill, one data file per screen, and `DECIDED.md` / `OPEN.md`. Everything factual is generated from files we already maintain. (1) The app team builds and owns the RN components and screens; we own the contract. (2) The October TSX and hand-off notes in `ui-screens/native/` are marked "don't use — see `handoff/`" once the kit is approved (backlog). (3) Pilot screen: Sign-in. Proposal: `docs/proposals/2026-11-09-native-handoff-kit.md` (§6 is the done-checklist). **Amended same day (Apurva):** built the D-95 fast-build way: Codex writes the checks from the proposal before the build and is the fresh-AI tester and the critic; tap-to-inspect at 390 only (360 as pictures); one evidence sheet; runs in parallel with the prototype build (own files only). (Numbered D-96: D-95 is the fast build.)
- **Why:** a new team on a new app. A token list says what exists but not where to use it; a PRD alone makes the engineers' AI ask questions that are already answered, because it can't tell decided from open. We are not on the same Claude account, so the kit travels as files in the repo, which their Claude, Codex or Cursor read on their own. Hand-written RN screens went stale within three weeks; generated files can't.
- **Rejected:** Figma Dev Mode (not our source of truth, no React Native names, paid seats); a Style Dictionary pipeline (native tokens are already generated); writing their RN components or screens; a per-screen token list alone (doesn't cover the components, which aren't exported to RN); shared Claude artifacts or projects (different accounts).

### D-95 · Fast build for steps 2–8: lift the approved HTML, test the logic, picture compare as a report — 2026-11-09
- **Decision (Apurva):** steps 2–8 are built in two terminal runs (A: steps 2–5, B: steps 6–8), aiming to finish today. The approved, audited HTML for every state is lifted into the app. The plan v3.2 build rules (B1–B14) stay. **Cut:** the per-state pixel gate (the compare runs once per run as a report, and only states over 1% different are looked at); per-session Codex loops. Instead, per run, **Codex writes the test cases and Playwright tests from the spec before the build**, and does one full critique at the end (Apurva: use Codex fully; tests written independently of the build); per-state tests for screens that only display (tests stay for gates, routing, outcomes, hold / retry, refusals, reopen); per-session screenshots. Brief: `docs/renders/2026-11-09-build-fast/TERMINAL-BRIEF.md`. Amends D-90 (plan v3.2 §5 and B15) for steps 2–8.
- **Why:** the screens are frozen and audited as HTML, so lifting the markup makes them right by construction. A prototype breaks in its wiring, not its pixels. Step 1's verification load (one session per journey, Codex each session, region-by-region compare) would put the rest at days, which is close to Engineering's own build time.
- **Rejected:** keeping v3.2's full verification for every step; a code-to-code (DOM) comparison (more work than lifting the markup, and lifting it is that check).
